      <h1>Battery Quality Control</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Technology Reference · Manufacturing · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Battery quality control is the test-and-sort system that turns a
        manufacturing recipe into a <strong>consistent product</strong>. The core gates: <strong>in-process
        checks</strong> (paste density, coating weight), <strong>formation and grading</strong> (the first charge,
        then sorting cells by capacity and resistance), <strong>leak and electrical tests</strong> at assembly, and
        <strong>batch audits</strong> against the spec. QC is what separates two factories running the same design.
      </div>

      <h2>The QC gates</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>What it checks</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>In-process</strong></td><td>Paste density, coating weight, plate/cell dimensions</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Formation &amp; grading</strong></td><td>First-charge profile; capacity and resistance sorting</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Assembly tests</strong></td><td>Leak checks, OCV, CCA/high-rate spot tests</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Batch audit</strong></td><td>Sample testing against the full specification</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why consistency is the product</h2>
      <p>
        The chemistry is fixed; the <strong>variance is not</strong>. Two identical designs diverge through paste
        density, curing time and formation profiles — small drifts that become big differences in capacity and
        life. QC's job is to catch those drifts at the gate where they occur, not at the customer
        (see <a href="/lead-acid-manufacturing/">lead-acid manufacturing</a> and
        <a href="/lithium-manufacturing/">lithium manufacturing</a> for where the drifts hide).
      </p>

      <h2>Grading: where lithium differs most</h2>
      <p>
        Lithium adds a step lead-acid does not need at the same intensity: <strong>grading</strong>. After formation
        and aging, every cell is tested and <strong>sorted by capacity and internal resistance</strong> so packs are
        built from matched cells — the factory-side answer to
        <a href="/cell-balancing/">balancing</a>, reducing the imbalance the BMS must later manage. The grading data
        also feeds the warranty decision: cells that fail the sort never reach a pack.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> In batteries, quality control is the product — the chemistry is a solved recipe,
        and the difference between brands is the consistency the QC system enforces, which is why manufacturing
        audits reveal more than any datasheet.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The failure a buyer meets in year three was usually a variance a QC gate missed
        in week one. Understanding where the gates sit — paste, formation, grading, leak test — explains why
        consistency costs, and why the cheapest supplier is often the one with the widest variance.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

